## P1 — Agentic Lite extensions: tools, MCP proxy, hooks, and audit

FerroGate should add lightweight agentic capabilities without turning the AI gateway into a full agent runtime or agent OS. The gateway owns tool boundaries, policy, auditability, and safe extension points; external agents and clients own multi-turn reasoning, memory, scheduling, and complex agent orchestration.

This replaces the earlier broad `ferrogate-plugins` / governance-migration scope with a smaller gateway-native extension model.

## Design principle

FerroGate is an **agent traffic gateway**, not an **agent runtime**.

Keep the first phase focused on:
- tool registration and explicit tool execution;
- MCP/tool proxying with allowlists and timeouts;
- small request/tool hooks;
- audit/event output;
- Admin visibility.

Do **not** migrate core auth/policy/billing/circuit-breaker logic into plugins in this phase.

## Scope & deliverables

- [ ] Add a lightweight extension configuration model:
  - `kind = "request_hook" | "tool_provider" | "event_sink"`.
  - `enabled`, `order`, `config`, and explicit permissions.
  - validation for duplicate IDs, invalid ordering, and unsupported sources.
- [ ] Add a minimal extension registry loaded by the gateway at startup:
  - builtin/in-tree extensions only for this phase;
  - no dynamic local-code loading;
  - no WASM host yet;
  - no webhook-hosted plugin execution yet, except event sink delivery if explicitly scoped.
- [ ] Add a `ToolRegistry`:
  - register tool schemas from builtin/tool-provider extensions;
  - list available tools;
  - filter tools by tenant, API key, route, and configured allowlist;
  - never expose provider secrets or gateway credentials in tool metadata.
- [ ] Add explicit tool execution:
  - `ToolProvider::execute_tool` boundary;
  - timeout, request ID, tenant context, and structured errors;
  - audit log for every tool call;
  - default deny when the tool is not allowlisted.
- [ ] Add an MCP tool-provider slice:
  - connect to configured MCP servers;
  - import `tools/list` into `ToolRegistry`;
  - execute allowed tools through the MCP server;
  - no agent loop and no auto-execution by default.
- [ ] Add a minimal request/tool hook pipeline:
  - `pre_request` / `post_response`;
  - `pre_tool_call` / `post_tool_call`;
  - hooks may observe, mutate safe metadata, or deny if configured as blocking;
  - extension errors are logged and isolated unless configured as blocking.
- [ ] Add an `EventSink` boundary:
  - emit extension/tool/policy/audit events to an internal audit sink or configured webhook sink;
  - include `request_id`, tenant context, extension ID, tool name, latency, outcome, and error code.
- [ ] Add Admin visibility:
  - list extensions, enabled state, kind, health, and recent errors;
  - list registered tools after allowlist filtering;
  - report MCP provider health.
- [ ] Include minimal builtin examples:
  - `tool.echo` or `tool.health_check`;
  - `event.audit_log`;
  - a no-op request hook for pipeline tests.

## Explicitly out of scope

- In-process agent runtime.
- Multi-turn agent loop.
- Agent memory.
- Scheduler or background autonomous jobs.
- Skills loader.
- Code Mode.
- Loading arbitrary local plugin code.
- WASM plugin host.
- Broad message bus.
- Migrating auth, policy, API-key rate limits, billing reservation/settlement, or provider circuit breakers into plugins.

These can be separate later issues only if they remain justified by gateway use cases.

## Suggested API shape

```rust
pub enum ExtensionKind {
    RequestHook,
    ToolProvider,
    EventSink,
    Custom(String),
}

#[async_trait]
pub trait GatewayExtension: Send + Sync {
    fn id(&self) -> &str;
    fn kind(&self) -> ExtensionKind;
    async fn init(&self, ctx: ExtensionInitContext) -> Result<(), ExtensionError>;
    async fn shutdown(&self) -> Result<(), ExtensionError>;
}

#[async_trait]
pub trait ToolProvider: GatewayExtension {
    async fn list_tools(&self, ctx: &ToolListContext) -> Result<Vec<ToolDefinition>, ExtensionError>;
    async fn execute_tool(&self, ctx: &ToolExecutionContext, call: ToolCall) -> Result<ToolResult, ExtensionError>;
}

#[async_trait]
pub trait RequestHook: GatewayExtension {
    async fn pre_request(&self, ctx: &RequestHookContext) -> Result<HookDecision, ExtensionError>;
    async fn post_response(&self, ctx: &ResponseHookContext) -> Result<(), ExtensionError>;
}

#[async_trait]
pub trait EventSink: GatewayExtension {
    async fn emit(&self, event: GatewayEvent) -> Result<(), ExtensionError>;
}
```

## Suggested config shape

```toml
[[extensions]]
id = "mcp-tools"
kind = "tool_provider"
enabled = true
source = "builtin"
order = 100

[extensions.permissions]
tools = ["github.search", "github.read_issue"]
network = ["api.github.com"]
filesystem = false
shell = false

[extensions.config]
timeout_ms = 30000
```

## Dependencies

- Existing auth, tenant, policy, billing, and audit surfaces remain the source of truth.
- MCP execution can depend on the future MCP slice, but the first tool registry should be testable with builtin tools.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] Gateway can load enabled builtin extensions from config.
- [ ] Disabled extensions are not initialized and do not appear as active.
- [ ] Tool registry lists builtin tools and filters by allowlist/tenant/API key/route.
- [ ] Explicit tool execution succeeds for an allowed tool and is denied for a non-allowed tool.
- [ ] Every tool execution produces an audit/event record with request ID, tenant, tool name, status, and latency.
- [ ] Request/tool hook ordering is deterministic and covered by unit tests.
- [ ] Extension failures are isolated by default and blocking only when explicitly configured.
- [ ] Admin API exposes extension/tool/MCP health without leaking secrets.
- [ ] Existing auth/policy/rate-limit/billing/circuit-breaker tests still pass unchanged.

## References

- Design direction: keep FerroGate lightweight and gateway-native; borrow only Hermes-style extension layering, not Hermes-style local agent OS behavior.
- Related epic: #23.
- Follow-up extensibility can remain in #29 after this lightweight core is stable.

